/* num32.yon — the package file of `num32`, under prelude/.
 *
 * The site C(Num32): the 32-bit numbers.
 *
 *   Objects  Int32 (i32) — the places of this directory. The compiler
 *            counts them: they are not listed.
 *   Arrows   the `morphism` below — the generators of the site.
 *
 * The topos name is not written (it comes from the file). The terminal is
 * not declared (every topos has one by definition).
 *
 * Why two packages and not two files. Precision is a world, not a type
 * parameter. If `Number32` and `Number64` lived in the same site they
 * would be two objects of the same category, and `Widen` would not be a
 * functor — a functor goes between categories. Like `Length` in Metric and
 * `Length` in Imperial: same name, two worlds, and the functor connects
 * them. The name carries the precision (Int32/Int64) because the name is
 * what travels in qualified symbols; the world stays the site.
 *
 * Good consequence: the `<64>` parameter is gone, and with it the
 * duplication of `width()` — the site knows its own width.
 *
 * No Number (f32) yet: a `place Number is f32` would compute in disguised
 * f64 today (the transport is uniform) — the same rounding lie that keeps
 * floats out of the Widen functor. It arrives with the intrinsic
 * mechanism, or in float worlds of its own.
 */
topos where {

  /* ─── The generators ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Few by choice: putting an arrow here costs, because every outgoing
   * functor has to translate it. The derived ones (gcd, ipow, bit_count,
   * abs…) are `fun` in the places, and a functor obtains them by
   * functoriality — F(g∘f) = F(g)∘F(f) leaves no choice, so they are not
   * declared.
   *
   * `uses Additive` / `uses Multiplicative` arrive with the algebra
   * cantiere. And `mul`, not `times`: `times` is a loop keyword
   * (`repeat at most N times`) and is unavailable as a name. */
  morphism plus(a: Int32, b: Int32): Int32
  morphism mul(a: Int32, b: Int32): Int32


  /* ═══ The Widen functor — integer widening ════════════════════════════
   *
   * The one genuine functor of the numeric tower, and its laws are true
   * because integer arithmetic does not round:
   *
   *     widen(a + b) == widen(a) + widen(b)      exact
   *     widen(a * b) == widen(a) * widen(b)      exact
   *
   * Floating point breaks them by construction: the sum in f32 rounds
   * before widening, and rounding does not commute with a change of
   * precision. That is why the functor covers Int32 → Int64 and not
   * Number.
   *
   * The form waits for the construct: `on object` / `on morphism` on a
   * functor does not exist yet (today a functor has parameters and a
   * single body — the redesign is on the ledger). When it does:
   *
   *   functor Widen from Num32 to Num64 {
   *     on object(n: Int32): Int64 { return widen_i32_i64(n) }
   *     on morphism plus  via plus_i64
   *     on morphism mul   via mul_i64
   *     law additive
   *     law multiplicative
   *     law composition
   *   }
   *
   * The implementation widen_i32_i64 already lives in Int32.yon (the house
   * is given by the target of the Int32 → Int64 arrows): decimal
   * decomposition, no intrinsics — integral literals bridge the carriers.
   */
}
